RED for Epoch68 UNIX V7
========================

This is the active, deliberately small port of Edward K. Ream's RED screen
editor. The untouched imported source and its documentation remain in the
archived UNIX768 repository's source-intake tree. The imported repository was
at upstream commit
2905b6eafb777baf9e073b23b8ccc846b64b2980.

The port keeps RED's editor core, 16-bit buffer representation, WordStar-like
control keys, line-block operations, search and replace, and the VT100 display
driver.  The CP/M, BDS C, Aztec C, ADM terminal, suspended-session, debugging,
and compiler-error-file integrations are not part of this target version.

Run:

        red file

The editor uses a 24-line, 80-column VT100-compatible terminal.  Control-C
enters command mode.  The arrow keys move up, down, left, and right.  The
original movement keys also work:

        control-E       up
        control-X       down
        control-S       left
        control-D       right
        control-A       previous word
        control-F       next word
        control-Q       page up
        control-P       page down

Useful editing keys:

        control-N       insert mode
        control-T       overwrite mode
        control-E       edit mode
        control-H       delete left
        DEL             delete at cursor
        control-Y       delete the current line
        control-U       undo the current-line edit
        control-L       split line
        control-O       join line
        control-C       command mode

Command-mode block operations use inclusive line numbers:

        copy first last after
        cut  first last after
        move first last after
        delete first last
        extract file first last
        inject file after

For example, "copy 2 5 10" copies lines 2 through 5 after line 10.  "cut" and
"move" are synonyms: they remove that block and paste it after the destination
line.  Destination 0 means before the first line.

Other central commands include help, find, findr, search, change, load, name,
save, resave, tabs, wrap, nowrap, clear, and exit.
